{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-lessons", "file": "references/session-output-shape-and-transfer-review.md", "content": "# Session review: invalid-grid feedback, unsupported transfer, and action discipline

In this instance, test dimensions were 21×13. The first guessed output was rejected as “not a valid ARC grid”; later candidate arrays appeared to have 21 rows and 13 values each, but continued to rely on unsupported stamping of the embedded 3/5 motif around every 8 marker. Demos were heterogeneous: some extended a small colored seed/object from isolated markers, while the last example replicated a multicolor stencil around marker locations. Their different colors and object geometry did not establish that the test's 8s select the seed copy operation.

Reusable lesson: after “invalid grid,” mechanically audit serialization (row count, every row width, palette and limits) before interpreting feedback. Then handle transformation semantics separately; satisfying the schema does not make a rule plausible. Use every fresh demo as immediate evidence before another action, and stop consulting overlapping references or calling no-op probes when they do not clarify the grid. Do not chain demo requests without reconciling the last pair. A previously identified session-specific reference already cautions that this task remained unsolved; no test transformation is established by this note.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review-lessons/references/session-output-shape-and-transfer-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-lessons", "file": "references/post-error-output-geometry-audit.md", "content": "# Post-error output geometry audit

When multiple complete grid submissions fail, do not treat later guesses as color/extent tweaks. Reassess the representation itself: compare input/output dimensions and orientations across all demos, determine whether outputs preserve the input shape, crop/project a region, or transform a boundary-defined structure, and only then infer cell content. Heterogeneous examples can encode different surface geometries for one abstract operation, but shared colors alone do not establish that bridge. Before another costly action, state what each demo actually supports and reject any candidate that cannot replay its full changed and unchanged cells. A useful decision gate is whether the remaining candidates predict different output shapes or differently transformed test markers; target a demo at that distinction or stop repeating the failed full-grid premise.

This is a recovery checklist, not a solved rule for the reviewed instance.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review-lessons/references/post-error-output-geometry-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/post-incorrect-reassessment.md", "content": "# Reassessment after an incorrect stream submission

An incorrect answer falsifies the complete inferred transformation, not merely a few predicted cells. Before the next action, replay each demonstration from its original input and output, recording all changed cells and meaningful unchanged candidates. Identify what the failed prediction assumed and which demonstration evidence rules that assumption out. Do not reuse the prior output with a few cell edits unless the demos independently establish that local correction.

For the next costly demo, name concrete competing hypotheses and the input feature whose possible forms would cause different outputs. If the hypotheses do not produce different test predictions, a demo has no decision value; if they do, request only when a new pair could distinguish them. Keep the one-action-per-turn contract, and only submit after the reconstructed rule predicts changes and non-changes throughout all available pairs.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review/references/post-incorrect-reassessment.md"}
